      "dom_id": "question-101048",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term ${a}_{3}=-9$ and the $8$th term ${a}_{8}=-19.$  Calculate the $11$th term.",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=-9$ and ${a}_{8}=-19$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-19-(-9)}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{5} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=-9,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ -9 & ={a}_{1}+2(-2) \\\\ -9 & ={a}_{1}-4 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-5+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5-2n+2 \\\\  & =-3-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =-3-2(11) \\\\  & =-3-22 \\\\  & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$",
